WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.040
Hey there.

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:02.220
Welcome.

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:06.440
This is our third show
from the Largo Theater.

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.540
And we're shooting from this theater.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:11.420
And what we think is a
very responsible way,

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:14.390
medically, I only have a
few crew members with me.

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:16.260
They're all socially distanced.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.050
They're all wearing masks.

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:21.090
Now, I do wanna talk
about something that is,

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.550
well, no way around it quite tragic.

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:25.370
As of this taping, as you know,

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:27.610
there have been over three million

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:29.510
confirmed cases of COVID-19

00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:34.510
in the United States
and over 133,000 deaths.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.530
Of course, each of those
numbers is a person,

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:39.630
and one of those people just happens

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:44.630
to be the mother of one of
my writers, Laurie Kilmartin.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.170
Laurie shared her experiences

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:51.070
watching her mother leave
this world on Twitter.

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:54.010
It's been getting a lot of attention.

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:55.240
Each of the tweets she wrote,

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:58.780
even the funny ones are,
of course, heartbreaking.

00:00:58.780 --> 00:00:59.870
I haven't seen Laurie

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:02.630
since we stopped filming at
Warner Brothers in March,

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:03.990
but I wanted to talk to her

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:08.423
and let her share her
devastating experiences with you.

00:01:09.509 --> 00:01:11.540
A lot of people were really drawn

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:16.540
to your very precise
way of expressing pain,

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:21.690
but also making it funny at
the same time and very human.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:25.780
And nobody does that quite like you.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:29.010
And some of the tweets were
really pretty remarkable.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:32.320
I was reading them, and as
your mom was passing away,

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:33.620
and I would find myself laughing,

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:36.223
but also understanding how
much you loved your mom.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:39.990
You walk this tightrope,
which is really fascinating.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:45.400
Yeah, I guess, like every comic,

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.860
you don't like to sit
in a feeling too long,

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.020
you'd like to pull yourself out of it.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:51.240
And the best way for us to do it

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.640
is write a little joke about it.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.810
And also, weirdly, I had already done this

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:58.113
with my dad when he died of lung cancer.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.410
At that time, I didn't
wanna leave his bedside.

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:02.830
He was doing home hospice

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:04.220
and the whole family was around him.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:05.840
So I was just sort of
tweeting little jokes

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:07.890
about what it was like.

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:12.837
And so, I have a specialty now,

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:15.580
and we'll probably never find love,

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:16.469
'cause why would you
wanna enjoy my family?

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:18.552
(laughs)

00:02:21.335 --> 00:02:23.900
But, so yeah, it was just a way for me

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:26.250
to at least find some meaning in it.

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:29.630
And also, I didn't feel
like people knew enough

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:32.290
what a COVID death was
like and how strange,

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:34.093
and surreal, and awful it is.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:39.283
Would you mind sharing a
couple of the tweets with us?

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:41.243
Yeah.

00:02:41.243 --> 00:02:43.777
Okay, here's a few.

00:02:43.777 --> 00:02:45.777
"My sister and I are both heartbroken

00:02:45.777 --> 00:02:47.997
"that mom's last words
to us were complaints

00:02:47.997 --> 00:02:51.753
"about the nursing home and
not about our appearance."

00:02:51.753 --> 00:02:53.687
(Conan laughs)

00:02:53.687 --> 00:02:55.417
"I just left a one-star Yelp review

00:02:55.417 --> 00:02:56.797
"of the skilled nursing facility

00:02:56.797 --> 00:02:59.420
"where my mom caught COVID-19."

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:03.160
Yeah, that seems like a
well-deserved one-star.

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:04.017
Yeah.

00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:06.017
"I was just notified that since my mom

00:03:06.017 --> 00:03:08.207
"is a Trump supporter who died from COVID,

00:03:08.207 --> 00:03:11.330
"her death has been ruled a suicide."

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:12.570
Oh, wow, okay.

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:14.381
(laughs)

00:03:14.381 --> 00:03:16.550
So, this brings up something

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:18.063
that I wanted to talk to you about.

00:03:18.063 --> 00:03:23.063
The COVID pandemic has somehow

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:27.430
improbably become now a political issue,

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:29.963
which really shouldn't be, I don't think.

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:35.900
But your mom was an avid Trump supporter,

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:38.560
and that was one of the areas of conflict

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:39.900
between the two of you,

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.190
but you sort of learned
to coexist with that,

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:43.410
is that right?

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:47.150
Yeah, I mean, you just,
and I think a lot of people

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:50.350
go through this just don't
talk politics with that person.

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:54.860
But also I regret that I let
it get in my head so much,

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:57.120
'cause I do think it affected

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:58.810
the last year or two I had with her

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:00.510
'cause I was so enraged.

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:05.340
I would blame her for policies
that she didn't know about,

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.950
when I looked at the
media, she was consuming,

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:09.750
she didn't know they were happening.

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:14.650
So yeah, that made things
more awkward for sure.

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:17.840
Your mom's situation was tragic,

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:18.900
all these deaths are tragic,

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:21.350
but your mom was negative for COVID,

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:23.050
you guys have been very
careful about COVID,

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:26.180
and then she had to go into a nursing home

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:27.370
where she contracted COVID.

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:28.330
Is that right?

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:31.720
Right, she was hospitalized
for shortness of breath.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:33.120
She was in poor health,

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.730
and she might have died this year anyway.

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:41.730
But after she was
removed from the hospital

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:45.520
and taken to a skilled nursing facility

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:47.780
so she could just get a little
bit of her strength back,

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:50.060
and that's where she caught COVID.

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:55.060
One person got it, and
then within nine days,

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:57.265
apparently they had 50 cases.

00:04:57.265 --> 00:04:58.310
It was crazy.

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:00.170
So, she was hospitalized.

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:03.973
And then after about a week,
she died at that hospital,

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:08.420
Maybe there are a lot of people out there

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:12.220
that hear about COVID, people
passing away from COVID,

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:15.060
but they don't really know what it's like.

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:20.060
It's, in your experience, a
particularly cruel way to die.

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:21.853
It's awful.

00:05:21.853 --> 00:05:24.890
And I have the comparison of my father

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:26.920
because he did home hospice.

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:29.370
It was nine days of him at home.

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:31.500
We were all with him the entire time.

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:32.740
We had friends coming by

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:35.300
to tell them what they loved about him.

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:38.780
It was like a parade and
a celebration of his life.

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:41.900
My mom didn't get to have any last words.

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:43.740
The COVID like took her to a place

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:45.963
where she couldn't speak
almost immediately.

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:50.200
She was alone in a room.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:53.240
The people that came
in to take care of her

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:55.260
were dressed head to toe in plastic.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:57.410
You could barely even see people's eyes.

00:05:57.410 --> 00:05:58.723
She didn't know them.

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:02.630
We watched on an iPad,
and we were also lucky

00:06:02.630 --> 00:06:04.920
that this hospital had
done a hotspot in March,

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:08.520
so, they had iPad set up in all the rooms

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:11.260
and we didn't have to share
a connection with anybody.

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:13.450
So, after we visited my mom,

00:06:13.450 --> 00:06:14.710
we headed back to my house

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:18.850
and we left the connection open
for 70 hours until she died.

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:21.150
And, she was just on my iPad

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:22.630
and I just take her around the house

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:24.230
and we'd be talking to her all the time,

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:26.810
and I put her next to
me when we fell asleep

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:29.060
and woke up and checked to see

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:31.640
that she's still breathing
and said hello to her.

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:32.517
We were just constantly saying,

00:06:32.517 --> 00:06:33.457
"Hey, we're here, Mom.

00:06:33.457 --> 00:06:35.057
"We're watching you,

00:06:35.057 --> 00:06:36.750
"we're making eggs."

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:38.117
This is when we just got a puppy.

00:06:38.117 --> 00:06:39.197
"This is our puppy."

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:42.790
We included her in
everything we were doing,

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:44.860
and I think that was so important.

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:46.280
And I hope that families

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:47.210
that are going through it now

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:48.780
get to experience something like that.

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:51.880
It's really important for
you to be able to let go.

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:54.770
And it's so important for that person.

00:06:54.770 --> 00:06:57.310
I mean, people, a lot of
the attitude has been,

00:06:57.310 --> 00:06:59.690
well, everyone's old anyway, so who cares?

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:01.230
Well, first, they're not all old.

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:02.410
A lot of young people are dying,

00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:06.963
and old people don't
deserve to go out this way.

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:10.400
It's awful.

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:11.640
It's not fair to them.

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:15.240
And it's so much of it's unnecessary,

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:17.603
if we'd just done the
right thing early on,

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:21.498
my mom might be alive.

00:07:21.498 --> 00:07:24.020
Maybe she'd be in terrible health

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:25.430
but she'd at least be here.

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:27.830
And if she were going to die this year,

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:31.930
just surrounded by us, and it
would be like a normal death

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:32.763
like my dad had.

00:07:32.763 --> 00:07:35.510
What do you say to a
lot of people out there

00:07:35.510 --> 00:07:36.910
who refuse to wear masks?

00:07:36.910 --> 00:07:38.163
What do you say to them?

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:44.130
You know what we should say
is a mask will protect you.

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:46.490
I think our huge mistake
was telling Americans

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:49.270
a mask will protect somebody else.

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:50.710
We're pretty selfish people.

00:07:50.710 --> 00:07:51.970
We don't care about other people.

00:07:51.970 --> 00:07:54.177
So let's pivot and just say,

00:07:54.177 --> 00:07:56.440
"A mask will protect you
from an awful death,"

00:07:56.440 --> 00:07:58.323
and maybe that'll turn things around.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:00.113
Yeah.

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:04.430
I have to ask this because
I've known you for a long time,

00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:09.180
should I, God forbid,
anything should befall me

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:10.830
sometime in the near future,

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:13.030
do you have jokes ready to go

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:16.000
for the death of Conan O'Brien?

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:18.210
Have you already written them

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:20.708
or do you think you could
access them pretty easily?

00:08:20.708 --> 00:08:21.650
Oh, yeah.

00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:22.610
The whole writing staff,

00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:26.161
we contribute to a Google
Doc every single day.

00:08:26.161 --> 00:08:28.576
We're so ready for you to die, Conan-

00:08:28.576 --> 00:08:30.270
(Conan laughs)

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:31.520
It's a shame you're still here

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:33.157
'cause the country really needs our jokes.

00:08:33.157 --> 00:08:35.980
(laughs)

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:37.443
You know what, I have to say,

00:08:37.443 --> 00:08:39.100
I've had a great life.

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:40.580
I'd like it to last a lot longer,

00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:43.440
but when I go, I would be honored

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:48.110
to have you craft some just amazing jokes

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:51.740
about my passing, because I
would like people to laugh.

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:58.390
Let's hope it's not
for at least 600 years.

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:00.730
Yeah, my kid has to get into college,

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:03.210
so I need him to go to college.

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:05.310
So yeah, let's keep you around for awhile.

00:09:06.230 --> 00:09:09.640
I'm really sorry that you lost your mom,

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:14.640
and in this way that,
frankly, just is terrible.

00:09:14.770 --> 00:09:17.390
And you know you're one
of my favorite people,

00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:20.820
and you're one of my
favorite comedy minds.

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:22.480
And also, you're such a good person

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:24.140
that you didn't deserve this,

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:25.570
your mom didn't deserve this,

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:29.250
but you have a lot of people around you

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:30.763
as you know that love you.

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:34.100
And we care about you.

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:35.245
And you're fired.

00:09:35.245 --> 00:09:37.912
(Laurie laughs)

00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:41.360
Maybe this is a good time,

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:44.833
maybe right after the death of your mom,

00:09:44.833 --> 00:09:47.210
and here on zoom, on television,

00:09:47.210 --> 00:09:52.210
but I'm just not liking
the work I'm seeing lately.

00:09:52.442 --> 00:09:53.820
(Laurie laughs)

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:55.810
It's been 16 days.

00:09:55.810 --> 00:09:56.970
I think it's plenty of time.

00:09:56.970 --> 00:09:58.400
I'm ready for it.

00:09:58.400 --> 00:09:59.950
I think I'm gonna get
a lot of public support

00:09:59.950 --> 00:10:01.570
for this move I just made?

00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:02.533
I really do.

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:05.900
Well, I miss seeing you in person.

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:07.290
I like our Zoom meetings,

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:11.060
and I wish you and your family

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:13.170
the best during this difficult time.

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:16.202
And like I say, "Yeah, you're fired."

00:10:16.202 --> 00:10:17.310
(Laurie laughs)

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:18.143
Thank you very much, Boss.

00:10:18.143 --> 00:10:18.976
I appreciate.

00:10:18.976 --> 00:10:19.809
Thank you.

00:10:19.809 --> 00:10:20.700
You're one of my favorite people too.

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:22.790
And you guys have been so supportive,

00:10:22.790 --> 00:10:24.270
and I'm so grateful.

00:10:24.270 --> 00:10:25.103
Thank you.

00:10:25.103 --> 00:10:25.936
All right.

00:10:25.936 --> 00:10:28.210
Well, take care, and I'll see you soon.

00:10:28.210 --> 00:10:29.686
Bye.

00:10:29.686 --> 00:10:30.519
Bye.

